ential that different guide  sequences could have stronger off target  effects because they are more tolerant  of mutations so I talked at the end of  the talk about whether some spacer  sequences are better than others in  terms of providing immunity because they  can provide broader specificity and be  less prone to having escaped mutations  on the flip side of that those same  types of spacers that are have broader  specificity which actually make for  worse guide RNAs for for genome editing  so I think that understanding really  with intrinsic specificity or the  intrinsic sequence features that lead to  these different levels of specificity is  important not only for understanding the  immune response but also for  understanding how we should design guide  or guide RNAs during genome editing  experiments  and how prevalent are crisper caste  systems in nature do all bacteria have  them yeah so that's a very interesting  question actually crisper caste is much  more prevalent in archaea than it is in  bacteria it's found I think in 85 to 90  percent of archaea that have been  sequenced today but there are estimates  that is found and only somewhere between  10 to 40 percent of bacteria so it  really depends on what we're considering  in terms of bacteria  I think it's found in about 40 percent  of bacteria that are culturable and has  been sequenced but if you also look at  uncultured bull so less studied bacteria  they actually tend to be a little bit  the enriched in some cases and the  implications of that are quite  interesting I think and it's a major  open question in CRISPR Cass biology why  are they so prevalent in archaea and why  are they so underrepresented in bacteria  there are I think some pretty obvious  reasons for why CRISPR cows may not be  what that widespread one obvious reason  is that there are actually anti CRISPR  mechanisms so phages have evolved  proteins that can kind of switch off  CRISPR defense mechanisms so these are  proteins that