ansfer so I think  that's a really interesting question  one that's very much still open in the  field as to why CRISPR Castes systems  are far more prevalent in archaea than  they are in bacteria okay we have time  for one more question you talked about  spacer acquisition but our spacers ever  lost if so how yeah so that is a  question that I get quite often  yeah so spacers are acquired I didn't  really talk about this but spaces are  acquired always at the five prime end of  the CRISPR array and that's a really  interesting mechanism because what that  means is that the most recent memory  that was acquired is the first crispr  RNA that's actually going to be produced  and it's been shown that in fact that  crispr RNA is generally the most  prevalent in the cell and that having  the space required at the beginning of  the array rather than somewhere in the  middle actually does have a much more  beneficial immune outcome so the cells  that have this spacer at the beginning  of the CRISPR array are much more immune  than cells that have the CRISPR  somewhere in the middle of the right and  because the acquisition is polarized in  this manner that means that the spacers  that are towards the three prime end of  the CRISPR array are much older and  could potentially be out of date  especially due to some of the things  that I talked about in my talk today  such as phage escaped so over time  phages are going to evolve it rather  rapidly spacers become sort of defunct  because they no longer have enough  similarity to the target region and  those spacers are the ones that tend to  get lost over time so it's a good  question whether or not spacers can get  lost because if they can't get lost and  you only have space requisition  occurring you're gonna have a larger and  larger CRISPR array and that could  eventually become very cumbersome for  the cell so it has definitely been  observed that spacers are lost and that  they're generally lost from the three  prime end